No — raising the pool size would just delay the same failure. The log shows `idle=0` at every sample while `waiting` climbs steadily (7→11→14→19→23→28) over the hour: connections are being checked out and never returned, not merely all busy at once.

The cause is in `db.js:12-14`:
```js
const client = await pool.connect();
const result = await fn(client);
client.release();
```
If `fn(client)` throws, `client.release()` is skipped and that client is leaked from the pool permanently. Over an hour that accumulates until `total` maxes out and everyone else queues (`waiting`) until `connectionTimeoutMillis` (2000ms) trips the errors you see at 14:04:11.

Fix: wrap in try/finally so the client is always released:
```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```
Increasing `max` would only buy more time before hitting the same wall.

