If the connection drops mid-query in `write()`, the `client.query(text, params)` call throws an error. The catch block then tries to ROLLBACK on a dead connection, which throws a second error that overwrites the original one. The route handler gets an unexpected error instead of knowing the write failed.

The database implicitly rolls back when the connection dies, but the code doesn't handle that gracefully. You should catch and suppress ROLLBACK errors since the transaction is already gone:

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    try {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    } catch (rollbackErr) {
      // connection is dead, rollback will fail — that's expected
    }
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

This way the original write error surfaces to the route handler, not the ROLLBACK error.

